<script is:inline>
  const SLIDES = [
    { name: "Opening", notes: "Thank you for the time. Russell has framed where I'm focused, so I'll get straight to it. I'm going to be more direct with you than I would be with the department heads later, because I think that's what's useful here. In about fifteen minutes I want to walk through what I heard, how I'd approach it, and what it takes. Then I'd rather spend the time on your questions." },
    { name: "Key themes", notes: "[Step 1, Time and expertise] I spoke with everyone on the leadership side and read through what came out of Russell's one-on-one cycle with the team. Four themes, in the order I heard them most. The first is time and expertise. Nobody has the time to work out what to build, and less to build it in a practical, scalable way. There's nobody with hands-on experience applying AI at scale, and it isn't anyone's job. And the hours go to manual work: event lists and follow-up on the go-to-market side, ticket updates and hunting for documentation in support.\n\n[Step 2, Documentation] The second is documentation as the foundation. It came up in every conversation, as a strength and a frustration. It's thorough, and when you need a quick answer you're out of luck. It goes stale the moment nobody's maintaining it, and stale is worse than none because people trust it. And it's what everything else depends on: support responses, vendor questionnaires, keeping ops in sync with releases.\n\n[Step 3, Where efficiency comes from] The third is where efficiency actually comes from. I split it three ways: customer-facing, helping employees do their job, and doing the admin work for them. In this industry I'd stay away from customer-facing first. The risk is too high and the phone is your differentiator. The opportunity is enabling the job, so people are faster at what only they can do, and automating the admin around it.\n\n[Step 4, Growth and cost] The fourth is the two numbers everything comes back to. Growth above thirty percent, with cash-to-close carrying it. And a lower cost to operate, as a growth play rather than a cost play. Every recommendation I make has to land in one of those.\n\n[Step 5, collapse] Underneath all four there's one thing, and it's the same thing Russell hears in his one-on-ones. Documentation is the foundation, and it sits upstream of nearly everything else.\n\n[Step 6, burst] Specifically. It's hard to use. Consistently difficult, and not just for the people who don't like Notion. It falls behind, and keeping it current is manual effort on the people doing the work, especially engineering. It's isolated from sources like your recorded calls, Salesforce, and transcripts. And there's no shared foundation, so AI efforts are starting in different places, for people and for AI.\n\n[Step 7, why here] So this is where I'd focus, for three reasons that came straight out of the conversations. Accessible: Russell's words were that anytime you need a quick answer you're completely out of luck, and what the team wants is both quick answers and real understanding of the product and the procedures behind it. Current by design: right now the ask is for engineers to go back and re-read every Notion page after every change, and the moment that doesn't happen it goes stale, which is worse than nothing because it gives false positives. This updates with the product instead. One context for people and AI: the same core feeds support drafts, the monthly call insights, marketing content, onboarding, and every workflow after, so it compounds instead of fragmenting. That's also why it comes before any AI use case. Most companies I work with start with the AI use case and build it on knowledge that's incomplete or out of date, and adoption stalls. A team can only be enabled by AI once it's enabled by its own knowledge." },
    { name: "How it works", notes: "[Step 1, Core] Today the core is documentation. It needs updating, review, and organization, and it isn't very accessible. Even the people who like it say that when you need a quick answer you're out of luck. What we do first is rebuild the core so it functions better and is accessible to the team: product and procedure docs still, plus the customer and transaction data behind them, tagged so it can be found, and organized by how people ask rather than how the pages were written. Then we turn it into a brain. Notion may well stay as the home for it. The point is what it becomes, not where it lives.\n\n[Step 2, Ingest] It stops being process documentation and becomes a smarter central hub for your data sources. Ingest brings them in and makes the brain smarter. Call transcripts, Salesforce, product documentation, marketing content, customer feedback. Engineering's module docs are another. We choose the first source to wire in during discovery, and Aircall is the leading candidate because it's already recording every call.\n\n[Step 3, Output] Output is where the AI-assisted workflows live, and each branch is a use case built on the same core. Customer intelligence: the monthly view of top complaints and feature requests from call data that Russell described. Support drafts: responses drafted from the knowledge and reviewed by a person, with the phone left to the humans. Product updates: release changes reflected in support and ops documentation without a manual pass. Go-to-market content and vendor questionnaires are further out. We build one in this project, and the pattern for the rest.\n\n[Step 4, Access] It doesn't live in isolation. Everyone has access to the brain, in the environment they already work in. Two things go in: human input alongside the ingested sources, and feedback on the brain itself, so what people know and correct becomes part of it. Two things come out: people find and use what they need directly, and they use and improve the output of the workflows downstream. Nothing goes out without a person until it has earned that.\n\n[Step 5, Loop] The last piece is what makes it intelligent, not just smarter. Ingest makes it smarter. The loop makes it intelligent: it reviews what's there, flags what's stale, connects new information to old, learns from how it's used and corrected, and keeps the core current as the product changes. Nobody is doing that by hand after every release. To the team it should feel like faster, easier systems, not replacement." },
    { name: "How we get there", notes: "Twelve weeks, two stages, and a review at the end of each. Each step produces what the next one depends on, and you see it before we move.\n\n[Step 1, Discover] Weeks one and two. I map how knowledge is stored, used, and connected today, rank the highest-value content for rebuild, and we agree on the current state and the priorities.\n\n[Step 2, Architect] Weeks three and four. Structure, metadata, ownership, access, the tools, and the first source and use case. Approved before anything gets built.\n\n[Step 3, Brain build] Weeks five through nine. The highest-value content rebuilt and current, connected to the team's tools, the loop running, the team trained. Done when the team reliably gets grounded answers where they work.\n\n[Step 4, Use case] Weeks nine through twelve. The first source wired in and the first AI-assisted use case live on the brain, with the team enabled and the pattern ready to repeat.\n\n[Step 5, Roadmap] Week twelve. A prioritized plan for the next sources, use cases, and improvements, focused on your biggest bottlenecks. Once the first source and the first use case work, the brain grows the same way: more pipelines in, more workflows out, on the same core." },
    { name: "What you get", notes: "[Step 1, deliverables] What you'll have at the end of twelve weeks. A documented current state and priorities. Approved architecture and a build plan. A working brain with team access and training. The first AI-assisted use case. A priority roadmap for the next builds. A knowledge ownership model, so it stays owned after I leave. And a repeatable pattern for every use case after this one. Every one is done when you agree it's done, and there's a review at the end of strategy and at the end of execution. This is about the team and how it works, so anything customer-facing, anything inside the product, and engineering's codebase documentation are outside it. From your side I need one decision owner, time from the people who hold the knowledge, access to the systems, and a technical partner for anything running in your environment. What I'm leaving behind is the pattern, so the team can repeat it with or without me.\n\n[Step 2, the numbers] Twelve weeks, at fifteen to twenty hours a week on average, for forty-two thousand dollars, fixed. It's sized from the time I expect the work to take. Software and infrastructure costs are separate. We build primarily with what you already have, and we'd review any options and costs together before any are incurred. On the return, I'd rather use the research than a promise. The baseline for knowledge workers is close to two hours a day searching for information and about five hours a week waiting for it or re-creating it. Even a fraction of that back across the team is real capacity, before you count what implemented AI with a roadmap does for how the company reads from the outside. Russell put it well. There's no budget line for this, it's about the return versus other uses of that capital, and that's how I'd want you to judge it. If you want to move faster, I have the capacity to increase my weekly time. If something changes internally, we keep the main thing the main thing and add to the scope in writing." },
    { name: "Questions and discussion", notes: "I'd welcome your questions. The links open the detail: the full process and timeline, the 8-week option against the 12, outcomes and the return frame, and scope with what Earnnest provides. Escape or Back closes a panel." },
    { name: "Thank you", notes: "I'd welcome your questions. When you're ready, the button picks a start date and it comes straight to me." }
  ];

  const TL = [35.3, 46, 67.3, 86, 91.3];
  const TL_T = ['t-strat', 't-strat2', 't-exec', 't-exec2', 't-wk1', 't-wk12', 't-s0', 't-s1', 't-s2', 't-s3', 't-s4'];
